course: dictionary
lesson: 3136
title: Eliminate: getting rid of what cannot be right
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To eliminate something means to remove it or get rid of it completely. In a maths problem, you eliminate the answers that cannot be right, so you are left with the one that can. «Ana eliminated 12 and 30, because the answer had to be odd.» Crossing out the wrong choices is called the process of elimination.
explain: In sport, a team that loses a knockout match is eliminated, which means it is knocked out of the competition. «Our team was eliminated in the first round.» The team has not vanished from the world; it is just out of the game. The clue is the phrase «in the first round», which tells you the team could not go on.
explain: Eliminate can also mean removing germs or waste, as in science and health. Ask yourself two questions: what is being removed, and is it removed completely? If the answer to the second is yes, eliminate means the same as get rid of.
fact: Eliminate is a verb: eliminate, eliminates, eliminated, eliminating.
fact: To eliminate means to remove or get rid of something completely.
fact: «Eliminate» can also mean to knock a team or player out of a competition.
fact: The «process of elimination» means solving a problem by crossing out the answers that cannot be right.
fact: In a spy or war story, «eliminated» can mean killed, so always check the context.
exercise: In maths, why do you eliminate an answer? | To write it in bigger letters | *To show it cannot be right, so you cross it out | To show it is the best answer | To copy it into your book
exercise: «Our team was eliminated in the first round.» What does eliminated mean here? | Given a prize for playing well | Asked to play one more match | Moved to a bigger pitch | *Knocked out of the competition
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to eliminate? | *Remove | Decorate | Measure | Whisper
test: Maya crosses out 5 and 9 as answers to a maths question. What is she doing? | Checking her spelling | Copying the answers out neatly | *Eliminating two answers that cannot be right | Adding two numbers together
test: Which sentence uses eliminate to mean knocked out of a game? | *The referee said the visitors had been eliminated from the cup | The coach asked the players to eliminate their doubts | The chef will eliminate the eggs from the recipe
exam: «The detective used the process of elimination to find the thief.» What does the process of elimination mean? | Writing a list of all the suspects' names | *Crossing out the suspects who could not have done it | Asking everyone to leave the room | Finding the biggest clue first
